In article <1991Sep04.183208.12785@convex.com> swarren@convex.com (Steve Warren) writes:
>
>I was thinking about wizards and how to make them depend on magic more.  I
>think that wizards should be able to "intrinsicize" their spells through some
>type of super-human effort.  The manner that appealed most to me would be to
>use the corpses of gray dragons.  For wizards only, eating a gray dragon
>corpse would randomly cause one spell (already memorized) to become
>"intrinsic".  Thereafter the spell would not wear out from using it.
>
This is an interesting idea.  Certainly there should be some more value
to eating the corpse of a gray dragon; at the moment, the gray dragon
and the yellow dragon corpses are the only ones that won't give you an
intrinsic (although polymorphing the first gray dragon corpse is a good idea!)

I don't think that your "spell intrinsic" should be limited to Wizards though.
I'm all for giving certain character classes "advantages", such as making
spells easier for Wizards, but just as a Wizard can still fight, a Barbarian
should still be able to cast spells and take advantage of whatever intrinsics
he/she may gain.

>Once an intrinsic spell is gained, it should only rarely fail.  But the
>wizard's ability to fight well should not increase as easily.  Don't make it
>harder for wizards in their first 10 levels or so, but their fighting ability
>should not increase much after that.  Once they begin to advance to the level
>where effective magic use becomes practical, their fighting capabilities
>should not match their magic capabilities.  They would naturally begin to use
>force bolts or magic missiles for attacks, rather than a Mjollner or other
>powerful weapon.  As they gain in levels, magic resistant monsters should
>become less capable of resisting them, in proportion to their level difference
>(this may already be true - I haven't checked the source for this).

I agree with all of the above (except the spelling of "Mjollnir" :-) ).
In particular, I believe Steve has hit on an excellent suggestion of
slowing the gain in fighting ability as the Wizard gains more magic power.
Intuitively, this seems to keep the game balanced more.

>
>I can see a special level being created for each character class.  A museum
>for the tourist, a palace (full of throne rooms) for barbarians, an gnome-city
>for elves, a giant temple for priests, etc.  Each special level would be
>designed to be particularly dangerous to the character class it is designed
>for, as well as offering special benefits for completion.

Interesting thought.  I think Elves should get a level chock full of nasty
Orcs, given their historical enmity.  Now the archaeologist should get
pits full of snakes, right? :-)

>
>How does this sound to other wizard players?
>

Very interesting; although I don't play a wizard anymore because it's
too easy.  Some of your suggestions would make it easier, true, but if
you cut the fighting strength enough and increased the usefulness of
the spells, it might be intriguing to play "W" again.

